WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.919 --> 00:00:09.798
So when people find out
that I study antidepressants,

00:00:09.839 --> 00:00:11.007
they often ask,

00:00:11.049 --> 00:00:12.342
how do they work?

00:00:12.884 --> 00:00:15.971
And people are surprised
to learn, we don't know.

00:00:18.014 --> 00:00:21.309
And part of that surprise comes
from the fact that we've been told

00:00:21.309 --> 00:00:23.603
that depression is a chemical imbalance,

00:00:23.603 --> 00:00:25.772
that you don't make enough serotonin.

00:00:26.064 --> 00:00:28.316
And we know that antidepressants,

00:00:28.358 --> 00:00:31.945
SSRIs are the most common
antidepressant prescribed,

00:00:31.987 --> 00:00:33.488
increase serotonin.

00:00:33.863 --> 00:00:35.740
And so they think that what we're doing

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.451
is we're fixing depression
by increasing serotonin.

00:00:39.411 --> 00:00:41.454
But that's not the whole story.

00:00:41.496 --> 00:00:43.790
In fact, that may not even be the story.

00:00:44.165 --> 00:00:46.126
What I'm going to talk to you today about

00:00:46.126 --> 00:00:47.752
is some of our work with ketamine,

00:00:47.794 --> 00:00:51.673
a really remarkable drug
that has rapid antidepressant effects.

00:00:51.673 --> 00:00:54.217
And it doesn't correct
a chemical imbalance.

00:00:54.259 --> 00:00:57.262
It actually causes the brain
to change, to adapt.

00:00:58.888 --> 00:01:03.143
So when we talk about antidepressants,
we first have to start with depression.

00:01:03.143 --> 00:01:04.936
And everyone uses the term depressed.

00:01:04.978 --> 00:01:06.146
We hear it all the time.

00:01:06.146 --> 00:01:09.649
"I feel depressed, I feel sad,
I have melancholy."

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:14.738
Creative expression
of depression is very common.

00:01:14.779 --> 00:01:18.366
For centuries, people have tried
to express the feeling of depressed

00:01:18.366 --> 00:01:19.576
or melancholy.

00:01:19.618 --> 00:01:24.164
For example, Picasso,
as illustrated in these paintings,

00:01:24.164 --> 00:01:27.917
went through his blue period
and his painting style changed.

00:01:28.501 --> 00:01:32.172
It really portrayed a sense
of sadness, of gloom.

00:01:33.256 --> 00:01:34.966
We know that writers do this.

00:01:35.008 --> 00:01:36.843
Poets, I mean, you can read a poem

00:01:36.885 --> 00:01:39.220
and literally be moved
to tears by the words.

00:01:39.763 --> 00:01:41.139
Musicians.

00:01:41.181 --> 00:01:43.224
How many sad songs do we hear?

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:47.687
Eric Clapton's "Tears in Heaven",
about the loss of his son,

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:49.064
is heartbreaking.

00:01:50.523 --> 00:01:53.652
But major depression is more
than just sadness.

00:01:53.652 --> 00:01:56.780
It's a serious medical condition,

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.281
especially left untreated.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:04.454
Depression is really
the dulling of emotion,

00:02:04.496 --> 00:02:06.456
really losing interest

00:02:06.498 --> 00:02:08.833
and sometimes completely withdrawing.

00:02:09.542 --> 00:02:11.586
The symptoms of depression can vary.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.715
Some people, it may impact more appetite,

00:02:15.757 --> 00:02:16.883
others sleep.

00:02:17.342 --> 00:02:21.096
It may have effects
on concentration, memory.

00:02:21.137 --> 00:02:22.305
A range of things.

00:02:22.347 --> 00:02:24.641
So not everyone has the same symptoms.

00:02:24.641 --> 00:02:26.893
But it's a serious illness.

00:02:26.893 --> 00:02:28.311
Again, left untreated.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:33.316
We know globally it affects
over 280 million people worldwide.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:34.943
So a huge number.

00:02:34.943 --> 00:02:36.695
Everybody in this room knows someone

00:02:36.736 --> 00:02:38.279
that's impacted by depression,

00:02:38.321 --> 00:02:41.157
whether they tell you about it or not.

00:02:43.535 --> 00:02:47.622
Moreover, depression is the leading
cause of disability in the US.

00:02:47.664 --> 00:02:50.667
Disability meaning really
loss of productivity

00:02:50.709 --> 00:02:52.335
both on the individual and family

00:02:52.377 --> 00:02:53.712
and on society.

00:02:58.007 --> 00:03:03.430
Part of the focus that comes
from studying depression, though,

00:03:03.471 --> 00:03:05.849
is really focused on antidepressants.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.184
And we all have heard of antidepressants.

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:12.981
And how this came about was really
completely randomly.

00:03:13.022 --> 00:03:19.279
It was noticed that certain drugs
had antidepressant effects,

00:03:19.279 --> 00:03:20.613
and as people studied it,

00:03:20.613 --> 00:03:23.658
they realized that if you
increase serotonin,

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.618
you had an antidepressant effect.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:27.912
So things like Prozac, Zoloft,

00:03:27.954 --> 00:03:30.206
common antidepressants,

00:03:30.206 --> 00:03:32.208
have been studied for decades,

00:03:32.208 --> 00:03:34.586
and we've seen ads on TV,

00:03:34.586 --> 00:03:36.254
in doctor's offices.

00:03:36.254 --> 00:03:39.966
People openly talk about the idea
we're increasing serotonin,

00:03:39.966 --> 00:03:42.427
we're correcting our chemical imbalance.

00:03:42.844 --> 00:03:44.179
But here's the thing.

00:03:44.846 --> 00:03:47.766
If you look at individuals
with depression,

00:03:47.807 --> 00:03:50.310
many of them don't have
decreased serotonin.

00:03:50.351 --> 00:03:52.020
They have normal levels.

00:03:52.896 --> 00:03:54.939
And that's a surprise to many people.

00:03:54.939 --> 00:03:57.859
This idea that we had
a measurable index of depression

00:03:57.901 --> 00:04:00.904
just because it was less serotonin,
many people talked about,

00:04:00.945 --> 00:04:02.697
but it really wasn't true.

00:04:04.115 --> 00:04:06.826
So that's been sort of
surprising to many people.

00:04:07.577 --> 00:04:09.829
But importantly,

00:04:09.871 --> 00:04:14.292
SSRIs still are incredibly
important treatment options.

00:04:15.126 --> 00:04:16.503
They help many people.

00:04:16.544 --> 00:04:19.506
They are life-changing
and they are life-saving.

00:04:20.632 --> 00:04:23.843
For many people, they really
give them the will to live.

00:04:25.053 --> 00:04:28.723
The thing about SSRIs
is that when you take them,

00:04:28.723 --> 00:04:32.060
you have this increase
in serotonin very quickly,

00:04:32.101 --> 00:04:34.229
but they take weeks to work.

00:04:34.979 --> 00:04:36.773
They don't work quickly.

00:04:36.773 --> 00:04:38.608
And so why do they take weeks to work?

00:04:38.608 --> 00:04:41.236
And that's the part
that we really don't know.

00:04:41.236 --> 00:04:43.029
But again, they increase serotonin.

00:04:43.071 --> 00:04:46.825
So if it was simply fixing,
it should happen quickly.

00:04:48.201 --> 00:04:52.455
And so while again, they're incredibly
important treatments,

00:04:52.497 --> 00:04:53.706
they are life saving.

00:04:53.706 --> 00:04:57.377
And if you're on an SSRI,
you should continue to take your SSRI.

00:04:57.377 --> 00:04:59.128
That's an important message.

00:04:59.128 --> 00:05:01.047
But that's why some of the research

00:05:01.089 --> 00:05:03.299
I’m going to talk to you
today about ketamine,

00:05:03.299 --> 00:05:05.385
which my lab and group
has been studying now

00:05:05.385 --> 00:05:07.637
for more than 15 years,
has been so remarkable.

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:11.432
Ketamine, this is
the structure of the drug.

00:05:11.933 --> 00:05:13.393
What's been noticed is,

00:05:13.434 --> 00:05:16.354
in a clinical study that had
nothing to do with depression,

00:05:16.354 --> 00:05:18.189
it was just an observation,

00:05:18.231 --> 00:05:19.816
they gave a low dose of ketamine.

00:05:19.816 --> 00:05:21.901
And some of you may
have heard of ketamine.

00:05:21.943 --> 00:05:24.195
At high levels, it's an anesthetic,

00:05:24.195 --> 00:05:27.031
at more mid-level doses
it's a party drug.

00:05:27.031 --> 00:05:29.701
But what they were doing
was a study with an incredibly,

00:05:29.701 --> 00:05:31.244
incredibly low dose.

00:05:31.244 --> 00:05:33.746
And for those individuals
that were depressed

00:05:33.788 --> 00:05:35.039
that received ketamine,

00:05:35.081 --> 00:05:37.750
they had a rapid antidepressant effect --

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:40.712
not within weeks, within hours.

00:05:40.712 --> 00:05:42.463
We didn't even know it was possible

00:05:42.505 --> 00:05:44.716
to have an antidepressant
effect that rapidly.

00:05:45.508 --> 00:05:47.927
And so people have been studying why.

00:05:48.261 --> 00:05:49.929
What is this rapid effect?

00:05:49.971 --> 00:05:51.806
Because it really is remarkable.

00:05:52.724 --> 00:05:55.935
And it doesn't just work in individuals
that could be depressed.

00:05:55.977 --> 00:05:59.939
It's really been studied in individuals
that don't respond to SSRIs,

00:05:59.939 --> 00:06:01.649
because not everyone responds.

00:06:02.483 --> 00:06:04.652
And so it sounds pretty remarkable,

00:06:04.694 --> 00:06:08.781
but it's important to mention up front
that ketamine does have risks.

00:06:08.823 --> 00:06:10.742
And as with any drug,

00:06:10.783 --> 00:06:14.329
if it's not used correctly
or given at very high levels,

00:06:14.329 --> 00:06:15.997
it can have adverse effects.

00:06:16.039 --> 00:06:20.877
So it's really, really important
that things are followed

00:06:20.919 --> 00:06:24.213
in a manner as they should be,
not just more-is-better.

00:06:24.255 --> 00:06:25.548
That is not the message.

00:06:26.257 --> 00:06:28.635
So we started studying ketamine

00:06:28.676 --> 00:06:30.345
and other groups did as well.

00:06:30.386 --> 00:06:32.722
And the first thing, which we all know,

00:06:32.722 --> 00:06:35.767
is that ketamine
doesn't work on serotonin.

00:06:35.808 --> 00:06:38.728
It doesn't increase serotonin like SSRIs.

00:06:39.479 --> 00:06:41.898
Instead, it actually focuses

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:45.068
on a different neurotransmitter
system called glutamate.

00:06:45.068 --> 00:06:48.821
And glutamate is what's important
in fast communication in your brain.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:52.867
Now what’s interesting
is that ketamine targets glutamate,

00:06:52.867 --> 00:06:54.285
but it doesn't activate it.

00:06:54.661 --> 00:06:55.912
It blocks it.

00:06:56.746 --> 00:07:00.750
So why would blocking fast communication
have any beneficial effect?

00:07:01.376 --> 00:07:03.753
And what we've been able
to show is that ketamine,

00:07:03.795 --> 00:07:06.172
by blocking this fast communication,

00:07:06.172 --> 00:07:10.677
what it does is it actually strengthens
particular connections in the brain,

00:07:10.718 --> 00:07:12.428
what we call synaptic plasticity.

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:14.389
It's just a change in your brain.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:17.058
Your brain is able to adapt
and strengthen connections.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:20.353
And we think that's what's driving
the antidepressant effect.

00:07:21.604 --> 00:07:24.899
And so as we've been studying this
and trying to understand,

00:07:24.941 --> 00:07:26.526
OK, what does this mean?

00:07:26.526 --> 00:07:28.152
How does this work?

00:07:29.112 --> 00:07:32.407
What we've been able to show
is that -- this is a nerve cell,

00:07:32.448 --> 00:07:36.828
this is one of the cells that we think
actually are strengthening connections.

00:07:37.453 --> 00:07:40.206
As we're studying this
and trying to understand it,

00:07:40.206 --> 00:07:42.375
it's really providing a lot
of different ways

00:07:42.417 --> 00:07:44.460
of how we're framing what is depression.

00:07:45.211 --> 00:07:47.880
So first of all, it's reshaping treatment.

00:07:48.673 --> 00:07:50.758
This is not a chemical imbalance.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:53.511
We are not correcting
a chemical imbalance.

00:07:53.511 --> 00:07:55.430
The idea that someone may be broken,

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:57.807
as people have talked about
with depression,

00:07:57.807 --> 00:08:01.769
they're broken, they can't make
an essential amino acid,

00:08:01.811 --> 00:08:04.731
essential transmitter like serotonin.

00:08:04.772 --> 00:08:07.108
It's not the case
because we're not correcting it.

00:08:07.108 --> 00:08:08.693
We're not doing anything to it.

00:08:09.068 --> 00:08:13.865
It's the idea that your brain can adapt
and ketamine is able to tap into that.

00:08:14.407 --> 00:08:17.493
So it changes the way
that we're thinking about depression.

00:08:17.493 --> 00:08:20.747
Moreover, it's changing how
we're reframing the timing.

00:08:21.205 --> 00:08:24.792
Instead of an antidepressant
requiring weeks to work,

00:08:24.834 --> 00:08:28.337
you can respond within hours,
which is remarkable,

00:08:28.379 --> 00:08:32.091
especially for individuals
that didn't respond to SSRIs

00:08:32.133 --> 00:08:35.511
that may have been depressed
without treatment for decades.

00:08:36.763 --> 00:08:38.723
And third, it's reframing hope.

00:08:39.432 --> 00:08:42.393
The idea that there are treatments,
that you're not broken,

00:08:42.435 --> 00:08:46.064
that this is just a matter
of getting your brain to respond,

00:08:46.105 --> 00:08:47.565
to respond to treatment.

00:08:48.149 --> 00:08:50.401
And that, I think, is a really
important message

00:08:50.401 --> 00:08:52.320
of how we're thinking about the future.

00:08:52.987 --> 00:08:55.531
As we continue to study ketamine,

00:08:55.573 --> 00:08:58.159
there are many different aspects
that we're looking at.

00:08:58.159 --> 00:08:59.327
And so, you know,

00:08:59.368 --> 00:09:01.913
with this diagram,
we're showing two nerve cells

00:09:01.954 --> 00:09:06.751
and just a neurotransmitter
in this case ketamine, actually,

00:09:06.751 --> 00:09:08.461
which targets the glutamate system.

00:09:08.503 --> 00:09:11.130
Glutamate is being released and activated.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:14.008
As we're looking at how it's sort
of causing this disruption,

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:16.260
this blocking of transmission,

00:09:16.302 --> 00:09:17.470
we're actually thinking,

00:09:17.512 --> 00:09:20.098
are there other ways
to trigger this plasticity?

00:09:20.431 --> 00:09:22.809
Some people are looking
at when you take ketamine,

00:09:22.809 --> 00:09:24.519
you have this window of plasticity.

00:09:24.519 --> 00:09:26.979
Do you respond to therapy better
during that time?

00:09:26.979 --> 00:09:30.775
Or what about to other drugs
perhaps brain stimulation.

00:09:32.151 --> 00:09:33.945
We also have been looking at it

00:09:33.986 --> 00:09:39.325
from a context of, can we extend
ketamine's antidepressant effects?

00:09:39.742 --> 00:09:41.327
So as we look at this plasticity,

00:09:41.327 --> 00:09:45.665
what's important to remember
is that it's like a painting analogy.

00:09:46.415 --> 00:09:50.002
If you have a blank canvas and you paint,

00:09:50.002 --> 00:09:51.546
you're adding something new,

00:09:51.546 --> 00:09:54.090
but then you can dial in the colors.

00:09:54.132 --> 00:09:56.342
You can make them vibrant,

00:09:56.384 --> 00:09:59.595
more brighter or less brighter,
however you want.

00:10:00.054 --> 00:10:02.765
Ketamine is not painting the canvas.

00:10:02.765 --> 00:10:04.767
It's not creating new memories.

00:10:04.809 --> 00:10:08.271
What it's doing is
it's alleviating that despair.

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:11.566
And some people describe, with depression,

00:10:11.607 --> 00:10:15.528
someone noted that,
before ketamine treatment,

00:10:15.528 --> 00:10:17.572
because they didn’t respond to SSRIs,

00:10:17.572 --> 00:10:22.368
that they were really living
in a dark room with no windows.

00:10:22.368 --> 00:10:23.744
And they couldn't get out.

00:10:24.328 --> 00:10:25.496
After taking ketamine,

00:10:25.538 --> 00:10:27.290
it didn't make it a party house.

00:10:27.707 --> 00:10:32.670
What it did is it made it seem like
the light was lifted and there was a door.

00:10:33.963 --> 00:10:35.339
And it provided hope.

00:10:36.716 --> 00:10:39.051
What ketamine is doing,
in the painting analogy,

00:10:39.093 --> 00:10:41.304
is it’s actually able to dial in --

00:10:41.345 --> 00:10:44.473
to lift the sort of despair, if you will.

00:10:45.057 --> 00:10:46.392
And that's important.

00:10:46.434 --> 00:10:49.937
So as we target this plasticity,
we think about how can the brain adapt,

00:10:49.937 --> 00:10:51.564
how can it change,

00:10:51.606 --> 00:10:53.024
one of the things we're doing

00:10:53.024 --> 00:10:55.693
is that this antidepressant
effect doesn't last forever.

00:10:55.735 --> 00:10:57.320
Like flowers in a vase.

00:10:58.112 --> 00:11:01.240
Ketamine treatment lasts
a few days and then it wanes.

00:11:01.741 --> 00:11:03.784
So one of the things we've been able to do

00:11:03.826 --> 00:11:09.373
is actually initiate studies
to look at giving ketamine,

00:11:09.415 --> 00:11:11.250
triggering this antidepressant effect

00:11:11.292 --> 00:11:14.128
and then can we sustain it longer

00:11:14.128 --> 00:11:17.590
so that you don't have to have
another treatment so soon?

00:11:18.591 --> 00:11:21.928
And we're doing this through
targeting, this plasticity,

00:11:21.969 --> 00:11:23.846
this adaptability in the brain.

00:11:24.096 --> 00:11:25.890
And we think that's quite powerful,

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:28.643
because again, it's not
about having to fix something

00:11:28.684 --> 00:11:31.812
because again, given all
the different symptoms,

00:11:31.812 --> 00:11:33.314
people have different levels --

00:11:33.314 --> 00:11:35.358
if it were a matter of fixing something.

00:11:35.358 --> 00:11:40.404
All we're trying to do is to tap in
to the brain's ability to change.

00:11:41.572 --> 00:11:44.158
And so where are we going with this?

00:11:44.659 --> 00:11:48.371
Well, ketamine, as I've told you,
is a really remarkable treatment.

00:11:48.913 --> 00:11:52.458
But it's not for everyone,
and it's not without potential harm.

00:11:52.917 --> 00:11:57.463
If you are currently taking an SSRI,
you should continue to take the SSRI.

00:11:57.505 --> 00:12:00.800
This is the first line
of treatment for depression.

00:12:01.175 --> 00:12:03.928
It's for individuals
that don't respond really,

00:12:03.928 --> 00:12:07.265
that have been most studied
in terms of taking ketamine.

00:12:07.306 --> 00:12:08.641
If you're interested in it,

00:12:08.641 --> 00:12:11.060
you should talk
to your health care provider.

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:13.854
But we're using ketamine
to try to understand, again,

00:12:13.854 --> 00:12:16.524
how do you generate
an antidepressant effect?

00:12:16.565 --> 00:12:20.027
And the idea that it's not
just an imbalance, again,

00:12:20.069 --> 00:12:22.238
that someone is not broken,

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:24.198
maybe their brain is just sort of stuck.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:27.159
And ketamine allows you
to adapt, to change.

00:12:27.702 --> 00:12:30.579
And that, again, is what
we think the response is.

00:12:31.122 --> 00:12:33.040
And so as we continue to study ketamine,

00:12:33.082 --> 00:12:34.250
we and other labs,

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:37.336
we're trying to understand
how can we make this better?

00:12:38.004 --> 00:12:41.632
How can we make this safer
to work for more people?

00:12:41.632 --> 00:12:43.759
And how can we sustain the effect?

00:12:44.135 --> 00:12:47.972
And I think those are all
really powerful messages of hope,

00:12:48.014 --> 00:12:51.100
because it's not just
about seeking treatment today,

00:12:51.142 --> 00:12:53.769
which is incredibly important,

00:12:53.811 --> 00:12:57.565
but also for our children
and our grandchildren and for others.

00:12:57.565 --> 00:12:59.567
Because this is a global issue

00:12:59.567 --> 00:13:02.820
and this is something
that there's a lot of work going on

00:13:02.862 --> 00:13:05.614
that's really creating a lot
of excitement in the field.

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:08.951
And it's also the power
of scientific research.

00:13:08.951 --> 00:13:10.119
Thank you.

00:13:10.119 --> 00:13:11.287
(Applause)

